# backup-widget data collectors.
#
# Three tiers, because they cost very different amounts:
#   1m   local log parsing only - no cloud calls, no filesystem walks
#   5m   one Dropbox listing call, for byte-accurate mirror progress
#   1h   one quota call per provider
#   6h   all three clouds plus a du over every dataset
#
# Per-second figures do NOT come from cron: the page queries rclone's rc server
# directly, in-process, which is the only way to move like a CPU graph.
* * * * * bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/duplicacy-status.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
* * * * * bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/rclone-live.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
*/5 * * * * bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/rclone-progress.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
5 */6 * * * bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/duplicacy-coverage.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
7 */6 * * * bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/backup-inventory.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
10 */6 * * * bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/backup-history.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
# Hourly is right for quota: it is three API calls, and free space does not move
# fast enough to warrant more. Offset from the 6-hourly tier so a quota check never
# lands in the same minute as a coverage pass competing for the same providers.
17 * * * * bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/cloud-quota.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
# Amazon Photos: reads the uploader's local ledger and status file only, never
# Amazon itself, so the 1m tier is cheap enough.
* * * * * bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/amazon-photos-status.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
# Google Drive clone: reads family-clone.sh's own log and state files, never
# Drive. The log is several MB, so the 5m tier rather than every minute.
*/5 * * * * bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/drive-clone-status.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
# After a reboot /var/local/emhttp is empty, so the 1h and 6h tiers above would
# leave the widget blank ("coverage check pending", no space left) for up to six
# hours. Once the array is mounted, run each of them once per boot. The marker in
# /var/run (RAM, cleared by the reboot) makes it one attempt, not a retry loop:
# a coverage pass that fails must not hammer mail.ru every minute.
* * * * * [ -e /var/run/backup-widget-warm-coverage ] || ! mountpoint -q /mnt/user || { touch /var/run/backup-widget-warm-coverage; bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/duplicacy-coverage.sh; } >/dev/null 2>&1
* * * * * [ -e /var/run/backup-widget-warm-inventory ] || ! mountpoint -q /mnt/user || { touch /var/run/backup-widget-warm-inventory; bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/backup-inventory.sh; } >/dev/null 2>&1
* * * * * [ -e /var/run/backup-widget-warm-quota ] || ! mountpoint -q /mnt/user || { touch /var/run/backup-widget-warm-quota; bash /boot/config/plugins/backup-widget/scripts/cloud-quota.sh; } >/dev/null 2>&1
